  "verbalization": "Hydrocortisone appears to be linked to Corneal Ulcer through a series of indications that suggest a potential mechanism of action. The evidence indicates that Hydrocortisone is associated with Iritis and Uveitis, which are inflammatory eye conditions, and these conditions are further linked to Fluorometholone and Medrysone, both of which are indicated to cause Corneal Ulcer. The shared pathway involves the use of corticosteroids, which are known to modulate inflammation and have a protective effect on the cornea. The presence of multiple pathways pointing to the same end point, Corneal Ulcer, supports the idea that Hydrocortisone may exert a protective or anti-inflammatory effect in the context of corneal inflammation. The integration of these indications suggests that Hydrocortisone could potentially be repurposed for the treatment of corneal ulceration, particularly in cases of inflammatory eye disease. The mechanism is supported by the common use of corticosteroids in inflammatory conditions, and the evidence from the dataset aligns with the known pharmacological effects of corticosteroids on inflammatory processes.",
